  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1253 PM CDT Sat Sep 5 2026\n\n17Z synopsis has a large ridge in the 300-700 mb range across much \nof the CONUS with a slow moving digging trough located in the \nPacific northwest.  A band of mid to high level clouds from last \nnight's convection continues from Elkhart to Hays.  At the surface a \n1005 mb low is located east of the Denver metro and the tightening \npressure gradient is leading to breezy south winds in western Kansas \nand temperatures are quickly warming into the 90s across much of the \nregion.\n\nTonight the main focus will be in southeast Colorado where a 700 mb \nshortwave will develop in the southwest flow and a cluster of storms \nare expected to develop in the 4-5 pm timeframe.  These storms \nshould slowly move east into far western Kansas after 7 pm and \ncontinue to migrate slowly northwest through the evening and \novernight hours.  Severe weather threat looks minimal as skew-t \nsounds have little 0-6 km bulk shear (around 10-15 kts) and high \nbased storms with DCAPE values around 1100-1200 J/kg and low level \nlapse rates at around 8-9 C/km.  CAPE structure above the LFC also \nlooks pretty narrow so the storms should mainly have a wind threat \nof some gusts to 50 mph and little to no hail threat.  A second area \nof thunderstorm activity will have to be monitored around the Hays \nto Great Bend area through the early evening as a few CAMs have \ndevelopment with a shortwave in the early evening however \nconfidence is low with widespread development and POPs are in the 10-\n15% range in these areas.  Storms will progress slowly into \nnorthwest Kansas during the overnight hours.\n\nGeneral trend during the workweek will be the continued stretch of \nheat.  Short and mid term models show little deviation in the upper \nridge across the central and southern plains and NBM forecast values \nof 25-75th percentile seem to have a 3-5 degree spread indicating \nhigh probability of temperatures reaching into the upper 90s to \nlower 100s.  Tuesday through Wednesday evening the upper trough in \nthe Pacific northwest will move into the northern plains.  This \ntrough is forecast to bring a weak cold front down into areas near \nthe I-70 corridor by Tuesday evening and stalling out through \nWednesday.  With the frontal boundary and modest lift in the mid \nlevels this will provide the next small probability of storms (20-\n30%) north of the K-96 corridor for Tuesday and Wednesday night. \nThis is subject to change if the frontal boundary can move further \nsouth so subsequent forecast discussion should make note if these \nchanges.\n\nEnsembles toward the weekend show a stronger trough in the northern \nplains that will drag a stronger cold front through Kansas during \nthe weekend.  The first taste of some relief from the heat could be \non Sunday with highs dropping back into the upper 80s and lower\n90s.",
